<h1 class="title">An Auditable Agent for Multimodal Biomedical<br>Representation Learning</h1>
<div class="author">Yangzhenyu Gao · BIOS 774 Project 3</div>
<div class="abstract"><h2>Abstract</h2>
<p>We present a rule-based agent that profiles paired modalities, chooses preprocessing and learning methods, runs validation pilots, freezes models and generates source-linked reports.
For PBMC RNA–ATAC, contrastive learning improves retrieval but reduces clustering agreement and adds no convincing accessibility-prediction benefit.
For breast histology and clinical data, a latent probe reaches macro-F1 {{ c.fusion_late_f1 }}, but gains depend on receptor covariates and the native head is weak.
The experiments demonstrate reproducible automation while exposing incomplete objective enforcement, inadequate convergence auditing and narrow foundation-model comparisons.
They do not establish generally superior multimodal representations.</p></div>
<h2>1 &nbsp; System design and agent decisions</h2>
<p>The task is to decide <em>how</em> modalities should be integrated, rather than replay a fixed pipeline. Typed data and objective specifications encode
modality type, sparsity, pairing unit, permitted supervision, downstream tasks and forbidden inputs. A deterministic registry supplies genuine adapters;
validation evidence selects a shortlist, and saved test evidence supports a conditional integration verdict. The reproducible core uses rules and
Jinja2/WeasyPrint reports, not an external LLM.</p>
<div class="figure"><img class="architecture" src="{{ architecture }}"><p class="caption"><b>Figure 1.</b> One orchestration graph serves both applications.
Content hashes, fit-scope records and failure receipts accompany every stage. Auditability does not imply that the implemented acceptance criteria fully capture the objective.</p></div>
<p><b>RNA + ATAC.</b> Exactly paired PBMC cells from one healthy donor [1] trigger training-fitted RNA normalization/HVG/PCA and ATAC TF-IDF/LSI;
MultiVI receives raw expression counts and binary accessibility. WNN, MultiVI, Gaussian MOFA+, self-supervised feature-dropout learning and paired InfoNCE
are eligible [2,5–8]. QC retains {{ c.pbmc_n }} cells ({{ c.pbmc_split|join('/') }} train/validation/test).
FixFlip cell types fit downstream probes, never the primary representation; they derive from WNN annotations [14].</p>
<p><b>Histology + clinical.</b> TCGA-BRCA diagnostic slides are grouped by patient [3,4]. Tissue/QC filtering and physical-resolution tiling precede
frozen patch encoders and attention MIL. Training-fitted clinical inputs are age, stage, histology and ER/PR/HER2 status, with missingness indicators.
Four-class RNA-derived PAM50 is the target; molecular target fields, outcomes and treatment variables are excluded from inputs. Labels may train supervised
fusion; image–table contrastive learning and actual local CONCH-Z tower tuning are alternative arms [9–13].
The cohort contains {{ c.primary_slides }} primary slides from {{ c.primary_slide_patients }} patients, with {{ c.labeled_n }} four-class labels.</p>
<h2>2 &nbsp; Selection and evaluation protocol</h2>
<p>Patient/cell splits precede fitted transformations; every patient's slides remain together. Small validation grids tune the same standardized logistic
and kNN probes across representations. An epsilon-Pareto rule over declared primary metrics, practical-equivalence filtering and simplicity select at most
two candidates for tuning. Final models are frozen before held-out evaluation. Classification, clustering, alignment, masking, stability and technical
confounding remain separate evidence categories; paired bootstrap intervals quantify test differences.</p>
<p><b>What this guarantees.</b> The protocol controls leakage and makes failures traceable. It does not guarantee sufficient optimization, equal effective
data access or correct translation of a prose objective into hard acceptance criteria. The results below distinguish these limitations from successful execution.</p>
</div>
