<h3>3.1 &nbsp; Objective satisfaction differs from selection</h3>
<p>The contrastive model traded slightly lower validation F1 within the predeclared equivalence margin for better retrieval. Test symmetric paired recall@10 rises from {{ c.ridge_r10 }} for the common PCA→LSI ridge reference to
{{ c.clip_r10 }}: difference {{ c.retrieval_delta[0] }}, 95% CI [{{ c.retrieval_delta[1] }}, {{ c.retrieval_delta[2] }}]. Macro-F1 gains remain uncertain:
{{ c.p_single_delta[0] }} versus RNA alone (95% CI [{{ c.p_single_delta[1] }}, {{ c.p_single_delta[2] }}]) and {{ c.p_concat_delta[0] }} versus concatenation
([{{ c.p_concat_delta[1] }}, {{ c.p_concat_delta[2] }}]). Leiden ARI is <b>{{ c.clip_ari }}</b>, below RNA PCA (<b>{{ c.rna_ari }}</b>) and concatenation
(<b>{{ c.concat_ari }}</b>). NMI is mixed (Table 1), improving over RNA alone but remaining below concatenation; it does not resolve the ARI regression.</p>
<p>The objective requested structure at least as good as the better single modality. The selector made classification F1 and retrieval primary, leaving ARI
secondary; it did not enforce structural noninferiority. The full PBMC objective was therefore <b>not met</b>, despite successful selection and a frozen
automated “integrate with caution” flag. This is an objective-to-criterion translation failure.</p>
<h3>3.2 &nbsp; Expression-to-accessibility prediction adds little</h3>
<table><thead><tr><th>RNA→ATAC predictor</th><th class="num">Cell AUROC</th><th class="num">Cell AUPRC</th><th class="num">Peak AUPRC</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for row in cross_rows %}<tr><td>{{ row.name }}</td>{% for v in row['values'] %}<td class="num">{{ v }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>
<p class="caption"><b>Table 2.</b> Existing held-out predictions; target ATAC input is masked. Cell and peak AUPRC average different ranking problems.</p>
<p>The learned RNA tower barely changes prediction relative to RNA PCA, while training peak frequency has the highest per-cell AUPRC. A small per-peak
AUPRC increase does not establish useful incremental prediction; no uncertainty estimate was saved for that increase. The row originally labeled as the
ATAC-only model's decoder actually calls its <em>RNA-side</em> encoder. RNA-only, ATAC-named and concatenation decoder rows are identical to RNA PCA and
are collapsed here. They are neither independent evidence nor ATAC-only predictors. Their retrieval scores also reuse one training-fitted ridge map;
this report counts <b>one alignment reference</b>, not three corroborating comparisons.</p>
<h3>3.3 &nbsp; Weak pilots do not establish inferior methods</h3>
<p>MultiVI was capped at {{ c.multivi_max_epochs }} epochs with {{ c.multivi_kl_warmup_epochs }} epochs of KL warmup. Worker logs reached the cap,
but saved curves are empty, preventing a convergence audit. Framework-default early stopping was disabled, although a custom validation callback exists.
The API default is 500 epochs [15]: this is a short pilot, not a fair converged-method ranking. WNN uses a muon graph, diffusion coordinates and kNN
out-of-sample interpolation. Training top-15 graph overlap with Seurat WNN is only {{ c.wnn_overlap }}. Compression, graph differences and extrapolation
can weaken this adapter; its F1 does not show canonical WNN is inferior. WNN-derived expert labels would otherwise favor a closely matched native analysis,
but do not guarantee that this different inductive embedding preserves their structure.</p>
</div>
