<h3>4.1 &nbsp; Receptor dependence limits the integration claim</h3>
<p>The selected representation concatenates attention-MIL image and clinical-MLP latents. Its probe F1 is {{ c.fusion_late_f1 }}, against {{ c.abmil_f1 }}
for image-only MIL and {{ c.concatenation_f1 }} for concatenation. Paired differences remain uncertain: {{ c.t_single_delta[0] }} versus MIL
(95% CI [{{ c.t_single_delta[1] }}, {{ c.t_single_delta[2] }}]) and {{ c.t_concat_delta[0] }} versus concatenation
([{{ c.t_concat_delta[1] }}, {{ c.t_concat_delta[2] }}]).</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Existing same-test-set comparison</th><th class="num">Macro-F1</th><th class="num">Her2 recall</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Image-only attention MIL + probe</td><td class="num">{{ c.abmil_f1 }}</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fusion + probe, all clinical variables</td><td class="num">{{ c.fusion_late_f1 }}</td><td class="num">{{ c.probe_her2_recall }}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fusion refit + probe, ER/PR/HER2 removed</td><td class="num">{{ c.without_receptors }}</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fusion native classification head</td><td class="num">{{ c.head_f1 }}</td><td class="num">{{ c.head_her2_recall }}</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="caption"><b>Table 4.</b> Receptor removal refits the same family and probe on the original split; it is not merely test-time masking.
Representation quality, receptor sensitivity and native-head behavior are distinct outcomes.</p>
<p>Without ER/PR/HER2, fusion falls to {{ c.without_receptors }}, <b>below same-test image-only MIL</b>. A receptor-independent integration benefit is
not supported. Receptors are biologically related PAM50 proxies and allowed clinical inputs, not prohibited molecular target copies. Their contribution
nevertheless qualifies task interpretation. This ablation shows dependence in this system, not a causal decomposition of every gain; a receptor-only
control and a paired interval for this specific comparison were not run.</p>
<h3>4.2 &nbsp; The probe is not the trained classification head</h3>
<p>Training averages the branches' class logits under unweighted cross-entropy. The native head reaches only {{ c.head_f1 }} F1 and recalls none of
{{ c.her2_test_n }} test Her2 cases. Her2 is a minority class ({{ c.her2_n }} of {{ c.labeled_n }} labeled cohort patients), so imbalance and branch-logit
averaging are plausible contributors; their causal roles cannot be isolated from these runs.</p>
<p>The probe fits a new standardized, regularized linear boundary on concatenated frozen latents, selecting regularization on validation. It reaches
{{ c.fusion_late_f1 }} F1 and {{ c.probe_her2_recall }} Her2 recall, showing that the latent retains information the native decision rule fails to use.
<b>The probe is also unweighted</b>; weighting does not explain its advantage. The native head is inadequate as a four-subtype classifier and must not
be described using the probe's score.</p>
</div>
<div class="paperpage">
<h1>5 &nbsp; What the agent learned—and failed to establish</h1>
<h3>5.1 &nbsp; Alignment is not a uniformly mixed biological manifold</h3>
<div class="modality figure"><img src="{{ modality_figure }}"><p class="caption"><b>Figure 2.</b> Saved RNA/ATAC tower display: fit training embeddings,
transform test cells. Visible offsets remain; UMAP is descriptive, not a new test or selection criterion.</p></div>
<p>The paired towers show a visible modality gap despite better retrieval. Ranking a cell's partner and merging both modality populations into a common
manifold are different requirements. The display does not prove complete separation, but it does not justify claiming full mixing. Together with lower
ARI and weak accessibility gains, it restricts the PBMC conclusion to alignment under the chosen retrieval criterion. Technical depth/QC remains
predictable; one donor cannot establish donor transfer.</p>
<p>For TCGA, source-site predictability remains a concern. A separately refitted site-held-out analysis reaches F1 {{ c.site_heldout }}; its different
test population prevents a paired comparison with Table 4. Exploratory survival C-index is {{ c.survival_c_index }}
(95% CI [{{ c.survival_lower }}, {{ c.survival_upper }}]; {{ c.survival_test_events }} test deaths), which does not establish prospective benefit.</p>
<div class="clear"></div>
<h3>5.2 &nbsp; Foundation-model and resource choices were conservative, not optimized</h3>
<p>UNI2-h and CONCH-Z enter production comparisons alongside ImageNet. Virchow2 and MUSK only passed loading/runtime checks, without empirical comparison.
Encoder choice reflects compatibility, feature size and compute defaults, not validation evidence that these encoders are best. Public-model pretraining
overlap with TCGA cannot be ruled out.</p>
<p>The cap of {{ c.tile_cap }} tiles per slide arose from two short-queue shards and a two-hour task limit. This was an operational choice, not an
aggregate-budget necessity: the saved projection for 256 tiles is {{ c.projection_256 }} GPU-hours, while project usage was {{ c.gpu_hours_used }} of
{{ c.gpu_hours_cap }} authorized GPU-hours. More sequential shards could retain the concurrency limit while covering more tiles. No larger-cap
performance was measured, so its benefit remains unknown.</p>
<p>CONCH-Z tuning genuinely updated pretrained parameters, but used only 128 training patients and four cached tiles each; inference used the first
single cached tile. Its low scores compare unequal effective data access and capacity, not the best achievable tuned foundation model. These arms
demonstrate execution capability, not competitive fine-tuning or superiority of frozen encoders.</p>
<h3>5.3 &nbsp; Automation strengths, failure modes and next design changes</h3>
<p>The agent orchestrates genuine adapters, leakage-controlled splits, validated resumes, failures, frozen representations and source-linked reports
across different pairing structures. Synthetic fixtures exercise serialization, true/shuffled pairing, a third modality configuration and objective-sensitive
planning. These software achievements are distinct from evidence of useful multimodal synergy.</p>
<p>The audit exposes decision-layer weaknesses: structural noninferiority was not enforced; reused references appeared independent; a decoder label obscured
its input; empty histories did not block interpretation; and resource settings were not revisited despite unused budget. A future agent should require
objective-specific acceptance tests, explicit baseline identity, convergence evidence, class-wise head diagnostics and comparable training data access
before recommending integration. These are proposed changes, <em>not evaluated repairs</em>.</p>
<h2>6 &nbsp; Conclusion</h2>
<p class="verdict"><b>PBMC:</b> retrieval improves, but structure-preservation and incremental RNA→ATAC prediction goals are not established.
<b>TCGA:</b> useful frozen-latent probe performance is conditional on receptors, without clear general integration benefit; the native head fails a minority
subtype. The unchanged automated flags say “integrate with caution.” This stricter report-level audit finds that neither experiment warrants an
unqualified claim of successful multimodal representation learning.</p>
</div>
